    <string name="certificates_algorithm_strong_explanation">%1$s uses SHA-256 or a stronger digest. No practical collision attack is known, though overall security also depends on the signing key type and size.</string>
    <string name="certificates_algorithm_sha1_explanation">%1$s uses SHA-1. SHA-1 is no longer collision resistant, so an attacker can create different data with the same digest. New signing certificates should use SHA-256 or stronger.</string>
    <string name="certificates_algorithm_md5_explanation">%1$s uses MD5. MD5 is not collision resistant and is unsafe for signing certificates. New signing certificates should use SHA-256 or stronger.</string>
    <string name="certificates_algorithm_md2_explanation">%1$s uses MD2. MD2 is obsolete and vulnerable to practical attacks, so it is unsafe for signing certificates. New signing certificates should use SHA-256 or stronger.</string>
    <string name="certificates_algorithm_unknown_explanation">The strength of %1$s cannot be assessed from its algorithm name. Key type and size may also affect its security.</string>
